<p>In the latest twist in a widening UK investigation, the British government is weighing legislation that could remove Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor (formerly Prince Andrew) from the line of succession. Officials say any move would wait until police inquiries end and would need coordination with other realms where King Charles III is head of state. The discussion comes after Andrew’s arrest and hours of police questioning on suspicion of misconduct in public office, tied to allegations that he shared confidential government trade information with Jeffrey Epstein while serving as a UK trade envoy. Police have kept pressing ahead since his release from custody “under investigation,” including searches at residences linked to him and outreach to former protection officers. Separate reporting said the Metropolitan Police is also looking into alleged use of London airports connected to revelations in Epstein files. King Charles III, in a statement, emphasized due process as the royal family tries to keep public duties on track while facing renewed scrutiny at home and abroad.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/lKyJ7Luo3fHihnW0MJs5dNQg0Yp1ceCmvp1PE7A5mD0GQDm5NuolD5jBYxTl20H_jGymH1Vsmi8rvB-ZpeJFQ-rxogkucgctfxBrCHkpgAvIeCZM5Pz96CPvoxMyTa3_fhSUbkqacJGic_Hc-my7leBZdM5D07aCnbU1PMuPR-azmMBMLDiBtE0Qwg' alt='Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor, brother of King Charles III, after leaving a police station on Thursday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Search activity: Authorities searched a mansion used by Andrew and reached out to security officers who previously protected him as they widened inquiries into the alleged sharing of confidential information.</li><li>Airports angle: Separate reporting said the Metropolitan Police was looking into suspected use of London airports after revelations in Epstein files.</li><li>Sandringham arrest details: Reports said unmarked police cars arrived early Thursday at Andrew’s home on the king’s Sandringham Estate—on his 66th birthday. Police questioned him for nearly 12 hours before releasing him under investigation.</li><li>Succession mechanics: Changing the line of succession would require agreement among parliaments in countries where Charles is head of state, officials and explainer coverage said.</li><li>Public image: A Reuters photo of Andrew slumped in the back seat of a vehicle after his release went viral and dominated front pages, highlighting the story’s global reach.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>King Charles III: In a statement, the king publicly stressed due process as police continue their investigation into his brother. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/keeping-calm-carrying-royal-family-weathers-worst-crisis-130358470'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>UK official (anonymous): A UK official said the government would consider new legislation to remove Andrew from the succession line once the police investigation is over, while noting the need to consult other realms. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3344185/uk-consider-removing-ex-prince-andrew-royal-line-succession'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Craig Prescott (University of London): A legal expert described the situation as a modern-era “fall from grace” for a royal and warned developments could continue as investigators decide next steps. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/prince-andrew-arrested-misconduct-epstein'>The Week</a>)</li><li>Thames Valley Police / investigators: Police said Andrew was released under investigation and continued searches linked to the suspected misconduct in public office inquiry. (<a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/02/20/police-continue-searches-at-andrew-mountbatten-windsors-residences-after-release-from-cust'>Euronews</a>)</li><li>Royal commentators (UK): Commentators told outlets the arrest and unknown next steps could test the monarchy’s moral authority and public standing, even as official engagements continue. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/